  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 532 AM EDT THU SEP 24 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens Friday evening with a closed ~572 dam\nlow off the Mid-Atlantic coast, nearly vertically stacked over a\n~995 mb surface low. An upstream 500 hPa ridge extends from the\nwestern Gulf Coast northward into northern Ontario. This is\nmirrored at the surface by a high pressure center extending from\nsouthern Quebec to over the Great Lakes. This setup will be \nkeeping dry, cool air feeding into the Coalfields on a light \nnortheasterly flow.\n\nThe pattern changes little through Sunday night--dry, cool air\ncontinues to feed into our region on a light northeasterly flow.\nWith 850 hPa temperatures holding not far from 10-12C and dew\npoints in the 40s, this will favor daily highs reaching into the\n70s in most places while nighttime lows dip into the 40s each night.\nSome of the coldest northern hollows (e.g., West Liberty, Sandy\nHook) could in fact dip into the lower 40s, per COOP MOS guidance.\nFriday night-Sunday night will almost certainly yield the coldest\ntemperatures eastern Kentucky has seen since mid-May.\n\nBy Monday, the upper-level low finally begins to fill in earnest and\ndrift away to the northeast while the 500 hPa ridge to our west\nbegins to shift east and break down ahead of another 500 hPa \ntrough translating through the western and central CONUS. \nConsequently, the aforementioned surface high pressure will shift\nfar enough east for winds to veer southerly and bring increasing \nwarmth and moisture back to eastern Kentucky for Tuesday and \nWednesday. That will mean not only the return of rain chances by \nWednesday, but also temperatures moderating back to above normal--\nWednesday highs are forecast to reach the upper 70s to mid 80s \nafter morning lows in the mid 50s to lower 60s.",
